              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mandy Schwanitz"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Ive been making this recipe for years now, the only thing I do different is actually use a whole bag of chocolate chips and a whole bag of butterscotch chips....sounds like alot but with a 13x9 pan it works well"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Anna"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great go-to recipe! Always a hit! I agree with the reviews, that not letting the sugar-corn syrup mixture come to a boil is the key to soft bars. When you see the first few bubbles, turn it off right away, add the peanut butter, and mix well. I also have found that about another 1/4 cup of peanut butter helps them stay chewier too. Awesome!",
            "datePublished": "2014-11-01T18:20:15.620Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Community"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have made this many times but use Kellogg’s Special K cereal instead of rice cereal. Might require a bigger bowl to mix it up to keep from crushing up the Special K too much",
            "datePublished": "2020-12-17T23:01:36.347Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Courtney Beth"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I added 1 tsp vanilla to the sugar, corn syrup and peanut butter mixture. I also used the entire bags of butterscotch and chocolate chips. Yumm!",
            "datePublished": "2020-05-29T19:25:15.040Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Elizabeth"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I thought this recipe was good but way way too sweet! I don't know if you need the extra sugar in the syrup. If I ever made these again, I would omit the additional sugar. Also, the people that are having difficulty with the end product being hard, you are cooking the syrup and sugar mixture too long. It should never boil as that is how you make hard candy. If you are unsure of what temperature is ok, try using a candy thermometer and keep it under the soft candy stage. Hope this helps.",
            "datePublished": "2012-10-08T15:48:52.253Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "rsanva"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This has been our family favorite for decades! I still have the original recipe square my Mother cut from the 1960's Rice Krispies box. Now the secret is out BIG favorite to take to any gathering. As others noted once the syrup and sugar start to boil remove from heat to complete mixing. They are a bit harder when boils too long, also I have been using a sheet cake pan instead of a 9X13 and by pressing them thinner makes more , just use a whole 12 oz bag of choc. chips &amp; butterscotch chips.",
            "datePublished": "2016-03-20T11:07:35.783Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Candee Krueger"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used 1 1/2 cups each of the chocolate chips and butterscotch chips to make sure it was covered..... easy and delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2020-02-24T23:17:19.157Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Baylee Benz"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I did it like this: \nPeanut butter and the corn syrup together. don't mix. Add rice crispy cereal to peanut butter and corn syrup. Melt chocolate and butterscotch chips together on the stove. Then pat down mixture in a pan of any size (depends on how big you want it to be). Pour chocolate and butterscotch mixture on top. Let cool. Eat",
            "datePublished": "2017-11-13T22:23:36.713Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Monique Page"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These are truly delicious. Instead of corn syrup, though I use organic agave syrup, which is, to me healthier, and instead of a whole cup, I use about 3/4 cup, and also 3/4 cup of sugar, and they are still delicious. Everybody always loves them, they go fast at potlucks and celebrations.",
            "datePublished": "2017-04-14T10:39:49.833Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Rhonda Z"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have also spread the cereal mixture into a rectangle, topped it with the melted chips and then rolled it up \"jelly roll\" style. Slice the pinwheels an inch thick and allow them to set up. Or you can freeze them in single layers divided by parchment or wax paper. This is a great make ahead party treat. Just set them out to thaw.",
            "datePublished": "2014-08-23T09:55:54.067Z"
          },
          {
